  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nAn expansive upper-level blocking pattern continues to govern \nthe regional weather regime, effectively sandwiching central \nIllinois between a deep coastal low (Nor'easter) tracking along\nthe Mid-Atlantic/New England seaboard and a shortwave trough \nlifting northward across the Great Plains toward the Upper \nMississippi Valley. Mid-level moisture advection associated with\nthe western shortwave is spreading eastward into the local \nforecast area, though it encounters a deeply entrenched dry sub-\ncloud layer across eastern Illinois, where gentle easterly flow\ncontinues to advect in dewpoints in the upper 40s.\n\nSubtle mid-level convergence atop this dry boundary layer will \nsupport scattered, high-based showers this afternoon and \nevening, primarily west of Interstate 55. Weak atmospheric flow\nwill result in slow-moving or training cell motion. This \nprolonged residence time over localized corridors could \nsufficiently erode the near- surface dry air to yield measurable\nprecipitation across our western counties. \n\nHighs today will feature a cloud- and precip- driven gradient, \nranging from the upper 60s west of I-55 to the mid-70s eastward \nwhere dry air and sun persist longer.\n\nFor Monday, persistent blocking maintains generally tranquil \nconditions across the region. Expect variable cloudiness and \noccasional light sprinkles, with seasonable high temperatures \nholding in the low-to-mid 70s under a light easterly surface \nwind.\n\nThe benign pattern will collapse rapidly by Tuesday as global \nguidance aligns on strong shortwave energy ejecting out of the \nSouthwest U.S. ahead of a broader parent trough. Warm air and \nmoisture advection ahead of this swift-moving feature will boost\nTuesday afternoon highs into the upper 70s. As the attendant \ncold front approaches, elevated ascent will saturate the column,\nbringing increasing rain chances from west to east Tuesday \nevening into Wednesday as the front slowly creeps into central \nIllinois.\n\nHydro concerns take center stage Wednesday through Friday as \nthe frontal boundary decelerates and becomes quasi-stationary \nacross the forecast area, orienting nearly parallel to the \nupper-level flow. Global deterministic guidance strongly favors \nthe southwestern upper trough phasing with Tropical Depression \nPolo. This tropical interaction will transport a deep reservoir \nof moisture northward along the stalled baroclinic zone.\n\nRepeated rounds of rainfall look increasingly probable \nWednesday through Friday as the core shortwave advances \nsluggishly along the wavering front. High rainfall efficiency \nand training showers will make localized flooding our primary \nconcern through late week, as weak instability and shear \nparameters keep the severe weather threat low. \n\nThe potential for heavy rainfall is well-represented in current\nNBM probabilities, which indicate a 25-50% chance of 72-hour \nrainfall totals exceeding 2 inches between Wednesday morning and\nSaturday morning. The highest probability axis favors areas \nnorthwest of the Illinois River Valley, with lower probabilities\nanchored over southeast Illinois. Precipitation chances should \nslowly wane into next weekend as Polo completes its \nextratropical transition and the main trough kicks east.",
